{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 47, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "Raymond III served as the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187 and he played a pivotal role within the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the twelfth century. His life involved complex political maneuvering alongside military campaigns against rising Muslim powers in the region. He acted as a regent for Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy and this position placed him at the center of royal succession disputes. Raymond often clashed with other noble factions including the Knights Templar and various courtiers who favored aggressive warfare over diplomatic solutions. His strategic mindset frequently urged caution while others pushed for immediate conflict with Saladin.\n\n*Early Life and Succession Challenges*\nRaymond inherited his title after his father died under suspicious circumstances involving the Assassins. He spent his youth consolidating power within the County of Tripoli while navigating threats from neighboring Muslim states. The young count faced internal dissent and external pressure yet he managed to secure his borders through alliances and strategic marriages. His marriage to Eschiva of Bures brought him additional lands and influence within the kingdom. This union strengthened his political standing and allowed him to exert greater control over northern territories.\n\n*Regency and Political Intrigue*\nThe count became regent for the young King Baldwin IV and this role defined much of his later career. He attempted to maintain stability within the realm while dealing with the worsening health of the monarch. Factional strife intensified as different groups sought to influence royal policy regarding the Crusader states. Raymond opposed the aggressive stance taken by Reynald of Châtillon whose actions provoked Saladin into open war. The count advocated for truces and negotiations but his voice was often drowned out by warmongering elements at court.\n\n*Legacy and Final Years*\nRaymond retreated from public life after losing influence at court yet he remained a significant figure until his death. He witnessed the disastrous Battle of Hattin where the Crusader army suffered a crushing defeat against Saladin. Some historians argue that his absence from key decisions contributed to the catastrophe. The count died shortly after the battle and his death marked the end of an era for the County of Tripoli. His legacy remains mixed due to accusations of treason by contemporaries despite his efforts to preserve the kingdom through diplomacy. Modern scholars view him as a pragmatic leader who understood the limitations of Crusader military power better than his rivals did during those turbulent years.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -7.5102134e-06}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " served", "logprob": -0.0009736985}, {"token": " as", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -4.768373e-07}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0027959654}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.046932243}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -9.536748e-07}, {"token": "2", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " until", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -1.3113031e-06}, {"token": " death", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -8.3446537e-07}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -8.3446537e-07}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -4.768373e-07}, {"token": "8", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -2.264979e-06}, {"token": " he", "logprob": -0.0023383996}, {"token": " played", "logprob": -0.01747427}, {"token": " a", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " pivotal", "logprob": -0.014431882}, {"token": " role", "logprob": 0.0}, {"toke